STATUS: ACTIVE

This addendum expands RC-CONTINUITY-05 and RC-CONTINUITY-06 to enforce full performance scalability, micro-distance spatial customization, layered memory streaming, seed-based world persistence, and automatic hardware optimization across all procedural and world-generation modules.

1. GRANULAR PLAYER-CONTROLLED RENDER DISTANCE SLIDER
- Micro-Distance Bounds: The player must have complete, real-time in-game UI slider control over active procedural generation and render distances.
- Distance Range: The UI slider must permit absolute custom input ranging from micro-distances (0.001 miles / ~1.6 meters, 0.01 miles, 0.012 miles, 0.013 miles, 0.05 miles, 0.1 miles) up to the maximum standard upper bound of 1.25 miles (2,011 meters).
- Real-Time Streaming Adjustment: Changing the slider must dynamically update World Partition and chunk-streaming boundaries instantly without requiring a game restart.
- Low-Spec / "Potato PC" Support: Setting the distance to ultra-low thresholds (e.g., 0.001 to 0.01 miles) forces the streaming engine to instantly purge all unneeded 3D meshes, AI actors, and collision volumes beyond the player's immediate personal bubble, freeing up system memory (RAM/VRAM) to allow smooth frame rates on low-end hardware.

2. DYNAMIC GRAPHICS SCALABILITY & AUTOMATED PRESETS
- Scalability API Integration: All environmental, mesh, particle, dynamic lighting, and visual effect modules must hook into a unified Scalability Profile System.
- Quality Presets: Engine directives must natively support user-selectable quality tiers ranging from Ultra-Low / Low ("Potato" Mode) up to Ultra-Realistic / Cinematic.
- Individual Subsystem Toggles: Scalability hooks must dynamically drive:
  • Resolution Scaling & Dynamic Upscaling (DLSS / FSR / XeSS)
  • Shadow Map Resolution, Cascade Distances, & Dynamic Shadows
  • Global Illumination & Ray Tracing / Lumen Intensity
  • Anti-Aliasing Toggles & Reflection Quality
  • Volumetric Fog, Atmospheric Effects, & Particle Density
  • Texture Quality Toggles & Mesh LOD (Level of Detail) Aggression

3. LAYERED MEMORY ARCHITECTURE & CHUNK STREAMING
- Active 3D Render Bubble: High-detail 3D geometry (mesh LOD0), dynamic lighting, active AI, full physics collision, and interactive objects instantiate strictly within the active rendering radius defined by the user's distance slider (or standard 0.8 meters to 1.25 miles).
- Complete Asset Unloading: Everything beyond the active render radius unloads completely from system VRAM and RAM. High-Detail Mesh (HLOD) distant vistas or skybox representations handle distant scenery without calculating physical geometries.
- Chunk-Based World Partitioning: The world grid continuously loads new grid cells in front of player trajectory and instantly purges unvisited/left grid cells from memory. Memory consumption remains flat regardless of whether the player explores 1 biome or thousands over multi-year play sessions.
- Seed-Based Procedural Memory: Unvisited or distant biomes exist strictly as lightweight mathematical formulas (Master World Seed -> Region Hash -> Biome Seed) and are only calculated into 3D assets when the character physically enters those coordinates.
- Fast-Travel Safety Queues: During high-speed flight or fast travel, the engine pauses full 3D rendering, queues lightweight data updates first, verifies surface placement, and instantiates 3D meshes only after velocity stabilizes to eliminate memory overloads and frame drops.

4. LONG-TERM WORLD PERSISTENCE & DATA SAVING
- Deterministic Re-generation: Terrain and biomes re-generate identically using deterministic seeds whenever the player returns to an area, incurring zero permanent RAM/VRAM cost.
- Delta Save Ledger: Only modified world objects (e.g., destroyed walls, dropped items, constructed structures, altered resource nodes) are recorded to a lightweight JSON/text save-file ledger. Unmodified terrain is never recorded to save files.
- Landmark Anchoring: Fixed canon lore landmarks remain locked to fixed global coordinates and stream in only when within physical proximity.
- Automated Memory Purging: Unused textures, collision nodes, and AI decision trees must be actively purged from VRAM the instant a chunk unloads to prevent memory leaks during long-duration game sessions.

5. HANDS-OFF AUTOMATED ENGINE SAFEGUARDS & HARDWARE PROTECTION
- Zero-Intervention Memory Safeguard: If system VRAM or RAM usage exceeds 85% capacity, the engine background thread must automatically drop non-essential LODs, purge distant cached chunks, and restrict dynamic procedural spawn queues to prevent crashes.
- Fallback Low-Spec Shaders: On ultra-low graphics settings, complex shaders (volumetric clouds, dynamic water displacement, real-time subsurface scattering) automatically convert to lightweight static textures and basic material passes.
- Automatic Spawn Culling: Any procedurally spawned entity, monster, or structure mesh that crosses outside the active render radius must instantly revert back to lightweight seed/state data in memory and un-instantiate its 3D mesh representation.

